  "final_text": "I recomputed the key numbers from the CSVs. My recommendation is **no, not yet**: fix CRAFT first, then decide on marketing. Both of the VP's arguments rest on a choice of definition, and neither holds up once you change it.\n\n## The two claims, checked\n\n**1. \"Units up 22.4% on August\"**\n- **What's counted:** CRAFT units in `demand_actuals.csv`, summed by calendar month.\n- **The problem:** September in the data has **5 weekly buckets** (02.09 to 30.09) and August has **4** (05.08 to 26.08).\n- **Raw numbers:** 7,488 units in August and 9,162 in September, so +22.4% month over month.\n- **Per week:** 1,872 units in August and 1,832 in September, which is **−2.1%**. Revenue per week is €825,278 against €824,848, so **flat**.\n- **Traced:** CRAFT revenue of €4,124,238 = `demand_actuals.csv` units × `product_lines.csv` base price, CRAFT SKUs, September weeks, 95 rows. This matches §2 of the report exactly.\n- **Caveat:** it's list-price revenue, and the data has no costs or margin (§6).\n\nThe report says this itself in §6: the +26% is mostly the extra week. For CRAFT, all of the growth is the extra week.\n\n**2. \"Defects fell from 3.21 to 2.51 per 1,000 units. No other line improved.\"**\n- **What's counted:** the number of NCR reports per 1,000 units shipped.\n- **The problem:** this counts reports, not defective units. The count stayed flat (24, then 23) while units rose because of the extra week, so the ratio fell automatically.\n- **Same data, measured by quantity:** in `ncr_records.csv`, `qty_affected` for CRAFT was 2,324 in August and 2,832 in September. Per unit shipped, that's **31.0% and 30.9%, so no change**.\n- **Scrap went up:** CRAFT scrap rose from 924 to **1,539 units**, from 231 to 308 per week. Scrap is the worse outcome.\n- **\"No other line improved\" is wrong by quantity:** NEST went from 11.8% to 10.5% and PULSE from 8.6% to 8.5%.\n- **Caveat:** NCR quantities measure production, and units measure shipments. The two populations aren't the same, so treat about 31% as a relative signal, not a defect rate. CRAFT is still about 3× NEST and 5× AURA.\n\n## Recommendation\n- **Decision:** don't move Q3 marketing budget to CRAFT now. Rework the line first.\n- **Why:** per week, CRAFT demand isn't growing (−2.1% units). By quantity, quality hasn't improved (31.0% → 30.9% affected), and scrap rose 67%. More demand means more units made on a line with about 30% NCR-affected volume.\n- **Risk both ways:**\n  - Waiting may cost some premium sales. The data can't put a number on that, because marketing budgets can't be assigned to a line (§4).\n  - Pushing now could scale up scrap and rework, and possibly returns later. The data can't measure returns.\n- **The VP's case, in its best form:** by report count and month total, CRAFT looks good (NCRs per 1,000 units 3.21 → 2.51, units +22.4%). That's only true with a 5-week month and counting reports instead of units.\n- **What should change the decision:** set this in units, not report counts, because counts can drop just by merging reports. For example: CRAFT `qty_affected` and scrap units both fall for several weeks in a row, measured per unit shipped over equal-length periods.\n\n**Two thresholds aren't set anywhere in the material:** what level of CRAFT `qty_affected` counts as \"fixed\" (NEST is at about 10.5%), and for how many weeks it has to hold. Someone in Quality or leadership needs to set both before this can be turned into a firm go/no-go rule.",
